{"type":"user","uuid":"01a1b6ed-369a-7f37-b812-858bcd56bf3a","session_id":"01a1b6ec-e364-796a-b544-183433e5f1dd","message":{"content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_00_LdMq5nL6QLcWmXNY7oES9522","content":[{"type":"text","text":"   1→import test from 'node:test'\n   2→import assert from 'node:assert/strict'\n   3→import fs from 'node:fs'\n   4→import os from 'node:os'\n   5→import path from 'node:path'\n   6→import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'\n   7→import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'\n   8→\n   9→import { enumerateTestFiles, findOrphans, verifyFullRun } from '../../scripts/run-tests.mjs'\n  10→import { platformTest } from '../helpers/platform-gate.mjs'\n  11→\n  12→const HERE = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\n  13→const ROOT = path.resolve(HERE, '..', '..')\n  14→const SCRIPTS = path.join(ROOT, 'scripts')\n  15→const RUN_TESTS = path.join(SCRIPTS, 'run-tests.mjs')\n  16→const CHECK_FAST = path.join(SCRIPTS, 'check-fast.mjs')\n  17→const CHECK_FAST_PS1 = path.join(SCRIPTS, 'check-fast.ps1')\n  18→const CHECK_DISCOVERY = path.join(SCRIPTS, 'check-test-discovery.mjs')\n  19→const CHECK_CI = path.join(SCRIPTS, 'check-ci.mjs')\n  20→const OK_FIXTURE = path.join(ROOT, 'test', 'fixtures', 'discovery-selftest', 'ok')\n  21→const SELF = 'test/unit/runner-selftest.test.mjs'\n  22→\n  23→function tmp(prefix = 'cliworker-selftest-') {\n  24→  return fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), prefix))\n  25→}\n  26→\n  27→function run(args, options = {}) {\n  28→  return spawnSync(process.execPath, args, { encoding: 'utf8', ...options })\n  29→}\n  30→\n  31→function output(result) {\n  32→  return `${result.stdout ?? ''}${result.stderr ?? ''}`\n  33→}\n  34→\n  35→function freshCiRoot() {\n  36→  const dir = tmp('cliworker-ci-')\n  37→  fs.mkdirSync(path.join(dir, 'scripts'), { recursive: true })\n  38→  fs.mkdirSync(path.join(dir, '.github', 'workflows'), { recursive: true })\n  39→  fs.copyFileSync(path.join(SCRIPTS, 'ci-matrix.json'), path.join(dir, 'scripts', 'ci-matrix.json'))\n  40→  fs.copyFileSync(\n  41→    path.join(ROOT, '.github', 'workflows', 'ci.yml'),\n  42→    path.join(dir, '.github', 'workflows', 'ci.yml')\n  43→  )\n  44→  return dir\n  45→}\n  46→\n  47→function writeMatrix(dir, matrix) {\n  48→  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(dir, 'scripts', 'ci-matrix.json'), `${JSON.stringify(matrix, null, 2)}\\n`)\n  49→}\n  50→\n  51→function readMatrix(dir) {\n  52→  return JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(dir, 'scripts', 'ci-matrix.json'), 'utf8'))\n  53→}\n  54→\n  55→function writeStep(dir, name, body) {\n  56→  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(dir, name), `import fs from 'node:fs'\\n${body}\\n`)\n  57→}\n  58→\n  59→const APPEND = (value) => `fs.appendFileSync(process.env.CW_LOG, '${value}\\\\n')`\n  60→\n  61→"}],"is_error":false}],"role":"user"},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b6ece74172df9c584a52539772f3","_messageId":"01a1b6ed1c5d781b9e4fca00263a9238","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b6ece74172df9c584a52539772f3","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b6ed1c5d781b9e4fca00263a9238","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b6ed1c5d781b9e4fca00263a9238","traceparent":"00-e2db99401a709910f956cc03bd750ebc-3271128b70b8e57d-01","baggage":"codebuddy.session_id=01a1b6ec-e364-796a-b544-183433e5f1dd,codebuddy.conversation_request_id=01a1b6ece74172df9c584a52539772f3"},"__timestamp":"2026-11-07T16:52:41.755Z"}
{"type":"user","uuid":"01a1b6ed-369e-7705-9f35-cf74cbc44cea","session_id":"01a1b6ec-e364-796a-b544-183433e5f1dd","message":{"content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_01_sFrWFYP6VWL5WBULGuYI1343","content":[{"type":"text","text":"   1→#!/usr/bin/env node\n   2→// Self-test for the test discovery rules: proves the runner notices drift, that a filtered\n   3→// manifest cannot stand in for a full one, and that decoys and orphans are handled correctly.\n   4→import fs from 'node:fs'\n   5→import os from 'node:os'\n   6→import path from 'node:path'\n   7→import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'\n   8→import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'\n   9→\n  10→import { enumerateTestFiles, findOrphans, verifyFullRun } from './run-tests.mjs'\n  11→\n  12→const HERE = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\n  13→const REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(HERE, '..')\n  14→const RUN_TESTS = path.join(HERE, 'run-tests.mjs')\n  15→const FIXTURES = path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'test', 'fixtures', 'discovery-selftest')\n  16→const EXCLUDED_PREFIXES = ['test/fixtures/', 'test/helpers/', 'test/perf/']\n  17→\n  18→function printErrors(errors) {\n  19→  for (const error of errors) {\n  20→    process.stdout.write(`FAIL ${error.id} ${error.path ?? ''}`.trimEnd() + '\\n')\n  21→  }\n  22→}\n  23→\n  24→function checkRoot(root, manifestPath) {\n  25→  const errors = findOrphans(root).map((file) => ({ id: 'discovery.orphan', path: file }))\n  26→  if (manifestPath) {\n  27→    let manifest = null\n  28→    try {\n  29→      manifest = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(manifestPath, 'utf8'))\n  30→    } catch {\n  31→      manifest = null\n  32→    }\n  33→    const verification = verifyFullRun({ enumerated: enumerateTestFiles(root), manifest })\n  34→    errors.push(...verification.errors)\n  35→  }\n  36→  printErrors(errors)\n  37→  if (errors.length > 0) {\n  38→    process.exitCode = 1\n  39→    return\n  40→  }\n  41→"}],"is_error":false}],"role":"user"},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b6ece74172df9c584a52539772f3","_messageId":"01a1b6ed1c5d781b9e4fca00263a9238","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b6ece74172df9c584a52539772f3","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b6ed1c5d781b9e4fca00263a9238","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b6ed1c5d781b9e4fca00263a9238","traceparent":"00-e2db99401a709910f956cc03bd750ebc-3271128b70b8e57d-01","baggage":"codebuddy.session_id=01a1b6ec-e364-796a-b544-183433e5f1dd,codebuddy.conversation_request_id=01a1b6ece74172df9c584a52539772f3"},"__timestamp":"2026-11-07T16:52:41.758Z"}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b6ed-3679-7e8e-9843-d13cc51b002f","session_id":"01a1b6ec-e364-796a-b544-183433e5f1dd","message":{"id":"01a1b6ed36797e8e9843d13af789e62a","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now I'll design the implementation. Let me create the task list and start with registry.mjs.\n\nKey detail: REGISTRY must have keys in order: schema, snapshot_version, codes, write_route_order, warning_codes, reject_reasons, draft_reason_mapping, forecast_reasons, risk, risk_basis, tasks_left_basis, thresholds, availability, identity, semantic_change.\n\nLet me write registry.mjs with the embedded data and functions.\n\ncompareRegistry: sections:\n- codes: keyed by code; changed if exit/http_status/retryable/trigger_id/trigger differs.\n- draft_reason_mapping: keyed by draft; changed if v1 differs.\n- thresholds.tasks, thresholds.fraction keyed by risk; changed if op or value differs.\n- Sets (value lists): warning_codes, reject_reasons.v1, reject_reasons.admin_only, reject_reasons.draft, forecast_reasons, risk, risk_basis, tasks_left_basis, availability, identity, semantic_change — element values themselves. removed/added based on membership; changed? For sets, \"内容不同 → changed 且键为 *\"? No — §3.5 says for write_route_order and thresholds.priority (ordered lists): content or order differs → changed with key `*`. For sets, keys are element values → removed/added. If order changes but same content? Sets are unordered; probably compare as sets → no change. Hmm, but deep equality could differ by order. Let's implement: sets compared as unordered sets of values; removal → removed:[\"warning_codes:X\"], addition → added. Order change with same membership → nothing reported (but then deep equality false... checkFrozen relies on deep equal, so freeze.version? no...). Actually checkFrozen's ok is deep-equal; errors list ids per compare. If only order changed, errors would be empty but ok false. That's acceptable per spec (ok iff deep equal). But it could make tests weird. Not tested. Keep simple.\n\nWait, for reject_reasons.draft, the elements include patterns \"level:<Level>\" — just string values, fine.\n\nOrdered lists: write_route_order and thresholds.priority — output changed with key `*` if differs (content or order).\n\nSorting: three arrays each sorted by code point.\n\nDuplicate keys? assume unique.\n\ncheckFrozen:\n- {ok, errors:[{id, detail}]}\n- removed → id 'freeze.removed', detail the item\n- changed → 'freeze.changed'\n- added → 'freeze.added'\n- version: \"除 snapshot_version 外两者深相等，但版本号不同\" → 'freeze.version'\n- ok iff deep equal.\n\nOrder of errors? Probably removed, changed, added. Tests only check presence.\n\nDeep equality: implement own deepEqual (no imports allowed in registry.mjs). Fine.\n\ngetCode(code) → entry or null.\n\ndecideGate(facts).\n\nWRITE_ROUTE_ORDER = REGISTRY.write_route_order (frozen array).\n\nDeep freeze REGISTRY.\n\nNote: snapshot must deep-equal JSON round-trip of REGISTRY. So REGISTRY must only contain JSON values, and key order preserved on stringify — JSON.stringify preserves insertion order for string keys. Contract keys like \"claude:...\" in goldens no issue.\n\nNow, careful: `codes` entries order of keys: code, exit, http_status, retryable, trigger_id, trigger.\n\nNow validate.mjs. Needs to be a mini validator supporting listed keywords. Let me carefully implement:\n\n```js\nconst ANNOTATIONS = new Set(['$schema','$id','title','description','$defs','examples','default'])\nconst SUPPORTED = new Set(['type','enum','const','required','properties','additionalProperties','items','minItems','maxItems','uniqueItems','minLength','maxLength','pattern','minimum','maximum','$ref'])\n```\n\ncheckSchema(schema) → string[] listing unsupported keywords as \"/properties/a: oneOf\". Need to walk: for each subschema location, check its keys. Path building: root path \"\" for root; \"/properties/a\". Format `${path || ''}: ${keyword}`? Spec: 形如 `\"/properties/a: oneOf\"`. For root, path is \"\" → `\": oneOf\"`? Hmm. Let's produce `${pointer}: ${keyword}` where pointer for root is empty string → \": oneOf\". Hmm, that's ugly but tests only need non-empty arrays and specific cases maybe. Test title \"every schema uses only supported keywords\" → checkSchema(schema) empty. And maybe test asserts format? In the tests I write, I can just assert emptiness. But the test \"validator reports expected path...\" also asserts unsupported keyword throws TypeError. I'll implement format as `${pointer === '' ? '' : pointer}: ${keyword}` maybe \"root: oneOf\"? Design says \"形如 `\"/properties/a: oneOf\"`\". I'll use pointer (empty for root) + \": \" + keyword. Keep it.\n\nAlso `$defs` is an annotation key ignored, but its members are schemas that must also be checked. Yes — walk into $defs values.\n\nvalidate(schema, doc, {loadSchema}): returns {ok, errors}. Must throw TypeError on unsupported keyword encountered during validation.\n\nImplementation: recursive validateValue(schema, value, pointer, depth, errors, ctx). Handle $ref by resolving.\n\nDetail semantics:\n- type: JSON types. integer type: Number.isInteger. number: typeof number and finite? just typeof number. null type.\n- If type mismatch → error {path: pointer, keyword:'type'}, and stop validating further keywords for that value (avoid cascading). Actually \"一个文档对一个关键字违规时不得连带产生别的错误（例如缺少必需属性不得再触发 type）\" — that refers to required: when required missing, don't also report type for the parent. Hmm, example: doc missing required prop; parent object type is fine anyway. The cascade concern: if required fails (missing property), the missing property isn't validated so no type error. OK.\n\n- required: array of names; for each missing → error at `${pointer}/${name}` keyword required.\n- properties: for each key present, validate against subschema.\n- additionalProperties: false → keys not in properties (and not matched) → error at `${pointer}/${key}` keyword additionalProperties. Note: if properties is undefined, all keys are extra.\n- items: single schema → validate each element at `${pointer}/${i}`.\n- minItems/maxItems/uniqueItems: at pointer.\n- minLength/maxLength/pattern on strings at pointer.\n- minimum/maximum on numbers at pointer.\n- enum/const at pointer.\n- $ref: resolve target; then validate with resolved schema (plus sibling keywords? JSON Schema 2020-12 allows siblings. Our schemas: handle-full uses $ref inside `call` property only. selection-ok models items is {$ref}. Keep simple: if $ref present, resolve and validate, ignoring siblings.)\n\nError dedupe: \"同一位置的同一违规只报一次\" — dedupe by path+keyword.\n\nDepth: recursion depth > 64 → throw TypeError.\n\n$ref resolution forms:\n- `#/$defs/<name>` → schema.$defs[name]; else throw TypeError? If missing, maybe error. Throw TypeError.\n- `<file>.schema.json` → loadSchema(file)\n- `<file>.schema.json#/$defs/<name>` → loadSchema(file).$defs[name]\n\nloadSchema provided by caller. If a $ref file is needed and loadSchema missing → TypeError.\n\ncheckSchema must also handle $ref? No.\n\nNow, schemas (21). This is the most laborious part. Let me carefully write each.\n\nFiles in contracts/schemas:\nwire (14): request.v1.schema.json, handle.v1.schema.json, handle-full.v1.schema.json, selection-ok.v1.schema.json, selection-failed.v1.schema.json, event.v1.schema.json, forecast.v1.schema.json, usage.v1.schema.json, pool.schema.json, model.v1.schema.json, inventory.v1.schema.json, config.v2.schema.json, session.v1.schema.json, error-envelope.schema.json\nspec (7): registry-snapshot.schema.json, surfaces.schema.json, migration-map.schema.json, golden.schema.json, golden-manifest.schema.json, ac-index.schema.json, agentline-forbidden.schema.json\n\nTotal 21. Names derived: `<name>.schema.json` where name is the identifier above. $id equals file name. Yes: \"$id（等于文件名）\".\n\nNow examples dirs names: exactly the 14 wire object names: `request.v1`, `handle.v1`, `handle-full.v1`, `selection-ok.v1`, `selection-failed.v1`, `event.v1`, `forecast.v1`, `usage.v1`, `pool`, `model.v1`, `inventory.v1`, `config.v2`, `session.v1`, `error-envelope`. C-5's expected sorted list: `config.v2 error-envelope event.v1 forecast.v1 handle-full.v1 handle.v1 inventory.v1 model.v1 pool request.v1 selection-failed.v1 selection-ok.v1 session.v1 usage.v1` — sorted by codepoint: config.v2, error-envelope, event.v1, forecast.v1, handle-full.v1, handle.v1, inventory.v1, model.v1, pool, request.v1, selection-failed.v1, selection-ok.v1, session.v1, usage.v1. Yes 14.\n\nNow validate.mjs is a \"contract\" module: no node: imports, no imports at all maybe? It may import nothing.\n\nscan.mjs, views.mjs may import relative. Keep them standalone (no imports) to be safe (purity test checks no `node:` imports).\n\nNow golden files and their content. Details matter for G-3 case counts:\n- quota-shapes.json 9 cases\n- b44.json 9\n- forecast/thresholds.json 21\n- forecast/quantiles.json 15 (n3×3, n4×3, n12×3, n20×3 = 12, plus n2-invalid, n1-invalid, n0-invalid = 15) ✓\n- forecast/confidence.json 11\n- forecast/burn-eta.json 14\n- capacity.json 5\n- policy/p1-order.json 1\n- p2-quota-low.json 1\n- p3-unknown.json 2\n- p4-identity-group.json 2\n\nTotal golden files besides manifest: 11. Plus spec files: ac-index.json, surfaces.json, migration-map.json = 3. Total 14 → F-1 `check-golden-frozen: ok (14 files)` ✓ and F-2 files.length 14 ✓.\n\nNow manifest registers contracts/spec/ac-index.json + surfaces + migration-map + 11 golden = 14. Good.\n\nNow docs/contract.md generation: tables etc.\n\nLet me now think about check-no-collab scanning: it scans lib, scripts, test, contracts, docs + package.json. My files must not contain \".collab\" literal. Careful: docs/contract.md includes manifest paths — no .collab. Test fixtures deliberately contain the string in violating files; they're under test/fixtures/no-collab but default scan skips `test/fixtures/no-collab/` prefix. Clean fixture files also skipped.\n\nNote: build the string via `['.', 'coll', 'ab'].join('')`.\n\nLine matching: read file as utf8, split into lines; count line numbers 1-based. Case-insensitive substring search of the needle.\n\nAlso must scan `contracts/examples` etc. Fine.\n\ngen-contract-doc: build from snapshot + manifest + schemas dir listing.\n\nDocument structure:\n```\n# CLIWorker 契约（自动生成）\n> 本文件由 scripts/gen-contract-doc.mjs 生成，请勿手改。snapshot_version: <N>\n## 1. 错误码\ntable\n<退出码 0 的说明另起一行>\n## 2. 403 触发与判定顺序\n...\n## 3. 警告码\n## 4. 拒选原因\n## 5. draft 到 v1 的原因映射\n## 6. forecast 枚举与阈值\n## 7. 可用性与 identity\n## 8. semantic_change\n## 9. 规格与金标登记清单\n## 10. 契约 schema 清单\n```\nF-15 checks headings exactly `# CLIWorker 契约（自动生成）` and `## 1. 错误码` etc. So headings must be exactly those strings, no trailing spaces. Good.\n\nF-14: renderContractDoc('.') === file content normalized; no \\r; no BOM; ends with single \\n.\n\ncheck-contract-doc: compare normalized.\n\nNow check-write-scope:\n- registry check always: unregistered files under contracts/spec/** and contracts/golden/** (manifest itself excluded) → FAIL write_scope.unregistered. manifest missing → FAIL write_scope.manifest_missing.\n- scope check when --scope/--scope-from given: requires --base else exit 2.\n  - changed = git diff --name-only --no-renames <base> + git ls-files --others --exclude-standard\n  - outside globs → FAIL write_scope.outside <path>\n- glob matching: * single segment, ** across.\n- --scope-from markdown front matter parsing.\n- success `check-write-scope: ok`; fail last line `check-write-scope: FAILED (<K> errors)` exit 1.\n\nF-7 `node scripts/check-write-scope.mjs` → registry check on real repo → ok.\nF-8 `--base 93ebbb3 --scope-from .collab/tasks/004/design.md --scope '.collab/tasks/004/**'` → ok. This means all changed files must match write_scope globs or `.collab/tasks/004/**`. Includes untracked files. Careful: untracked files include `.collab/worktrees/`? .gitignore has `.collab/worktrees/`. In the worktree, is `.collab` at repo root containing tasks? The working dir is the worktree root and `.collab/tasks/004/...` exists relative to it. Hmm, `.collab/worktrees/` is the gitignore entry; our root is `.../.collab/worktrees/004`. Within it there's `.collab/tasks/004/`. So `.collab/worktrees/` pattern doesn't ignore `.collab/tasks/`. So untracked includes `.collab/tasks/004/design.md` etc. That's why F-8 passes `--scope '.collab/tasks/004/**'`.\n\nBut wait: `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` run in the worktree lists untracked files relative to worktree root: `.collab/tasks/004/brief.md`, etc. Matches `.collab/tasks/004/**`. Good.\n\nAlso impl.md at root — that's outside write_scope! F-8 would report `FAIL write_scope.outside impl.md`. Hmm! The task says produce impl.md at ... where? \"产出：impl.md\" - presumably `.collab/tasks/004/impl.md`? The instruction: \"产出：impl.md、以及 design.md 明确要求创建/修改的文件（只能创建或修改这些文件）\". impl.md likely goes to `.collab/tasks/004/impl.md` (alongside brief/design/acceptance). That's under `.collab/tasks/004/**` → allowed by F-8 extra scope. Good. I'll write `.collab/tasks/004/impl.md`.\n\nNow check-golden-frozen: reads manifest, walks contracts/spec/** and contracts/golden/**, compares normalized sha256. Excludes manifest.json itself. Errors:\n- FAIL golden.changed <path>\n- FAIL golden.missing <path>\n- FAIL golden.unregistered <path>\n- FAIL golden.manifest_invalid <detail>\nSuccess `check-golden-frozen: ok (<N> files)`, exit 0. Failure last line `check-golden-frozen: FAILED (<K> errors)` exit 1. --write.\n\nManifest validation via golden-manifest schema? \"或登记路径不在 contracts/spec/ 与 contracts/golden/ 之下，或登记了 manifest 自身\" → manifest_invalid. I'll use validate.mjs with loadSchema. But check scripts read files, that's allowed.\n\nOrdering of walk: sort by codepoint.\n\nNow snapshot-update: dynamic import of `<root>/lib/contract/registry.mjs` (pathToFileURL). compareRegistry taken from the script's own repo registry (`../lib/contract/registry.mjs` relative to script). Read snapshot from root.\n\nCases:\n1. snapshot missing: write only if REGISTRY.snapshot_version === 1 → write, print `snapshot-update: wrote snapshot_version 1`, exit 0. If !==1? unspecified; probably fail. I'll treat as `FAIL snapshot.version_not_bumped have=<x> need=1`? Hmm risky. Design table only says \"仅当 ... === 1 时写出\". For else, I'll print `FAIL snapshot.version_not_bumped have=<v> need=1` exit 1. Tests I write won't cover that.\n2. deep equal → unchanged.\n3. removed/changed → `FAIL snapshot.breaking removed=<n> changed=<n>` exit 1.\n4. no removed/changed, no added, not equal → `FAIL snapshot.no_semantic_change` exit 1.\n5. added and version !== old+1 → `FAIL snapshot.version_not_bumped have=<a> need=<b>` exit 1.\n6. added and version === old+1 → write.\n7. arg error → usage, exit 2.\n\nNote ordering: check removed/changed before added (case with added+removed → breaking).\n\nDeep equal: use JSON.stringify(REGISTRY) === JSON.stringify(snapshot)? Key order matters in stringify but deep equal should be structural. Use a deepEqual helper in script (or imported from registry? registry doesn't export deepEqual). I'll implement locally.\n\nNow the tests. Five files with exact titles. These are the crux for A/B. Let me plan each test carefully.\n\n### registry.test.mjs\nTitles:\n1. `[AC-009] REGISTRY deep-equals contracts/registry.snapshot.json` — deepStrictEqual(JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(REGISTRY)), JSON.parse(snapshot)) — note JSON.parse(JSON.stringify) handles frozen.\n2. every code has exit, http_status, retryable, trigger_id and trigger — type checks.\n3. trigger_id and trigger text unique.\n4. codes, exits and retryable flags match the frozen exit-code table — embed 30 [code, exit] pairs + retryable set.\n5. five 403 codes mutually exclusive — enumerate decideGate combos; assert at most one code per combo (trivially one), each of five appears ≥1, E_READONLY/E_CSRF only when session_valid, E_ADMIN_REQUIRED only when !session_valid; trigger_ids and exit/http.\n6. write route order — WRITE_ROUTE_ORDER deep equals ['session','readonly','csrf','body'] and snapshot.write_route_order; decideGate precedence.\n7. resolveView applies the select view rules.\n8. lib/contract modules are pure and import nothing — static scan.\n\nNote: registry.mjs must import nothing, so the test imports REGISTRY etc. Test uses fs/path (allowed in test).\n\nresolveView test: cli full → E_VIEW_FORBIDDEN etc.\n\n### examples.test.mjs\nTitles 9-15.\n- every schema uses only supported keywords: for each schema file: checkSchema(parse) → [].\n- every valid example passes its schema.\n- every invalid example fails with exactly the declared path and keyword: errors.length === 1 and path/keyword match.\n- every wire schema has one valid and two invalid examples.\n- schema enums for codes, warnings and reject reasons equal the registry: gather all enum arrays in the schema named by keyword... The spec: \"schema 中 `reason`、`warnings[].code`、`error.code` 的 `enum` 与注册表对应列表集合相等\". Implementation: recursively walk schema collecting enums that are subsets of registry lists; assert each registry list (codes 30, warning_codes 8, reject v1 15) is fully covered by some enum(s) in the relevant schemas. Simpler: collect all enum arrays from all wire schemas; for each registry list assert exists an enum array in selection-ok.v1 (reject v1), selection-failed/error-envelope (codes), warnings (warning_codes) that equals it. I'll do targeted: load selection-ok.v1 schema, find properties.rejected.items.properties.reason.enum → compare with reject_reasons.v1 (set equality). selection-ok warnings items code enum === warning_codes. And error code enums in selection-failed.v1 and error-envelope === codes. Good deterministic.\n- validator reports the expected path for each supported keyword: table-driven. Each supported keyword at least one violation case; assert path & keyword; plus unsupported keyword throws TypeError.\n- examples carry no token-like strings: scan all example files for patterns `sk-`, `Bearer `, `ghp_`, `xox`, `AKIA`, 40+ hex, base64 40+.\n\n### freeze.test.mjs\nTitles 16-25.\n\n### scan-canary.test.mjs\nTitles 26-35.\n\n### spec-tracked.test.mjs\nTitles 36-51.\n\nBig effort. Let me now write files. I'll start with registry.mjs and the snapshot.\n\nFor the snapshot, easier: write registry.mjs, then run snapshot-update (writes file). That requires running node: allowed.\n\nLet's write registry.mjs now. Must be careful with exact strings.\n\nTrigger text list from design §3.3 — copy exactly.\n\nNow decideGate route set: 'public','select','bootstrap','admin_read','admin_write'.\n\ncompareRegistry implementation details:\n\n```js\nconst LIST_SECTIONS = [\n  ['warning_codes', r => r.warning_codes],\n  ['reject_reasons.v1', r => r.reject_reasons.v1],\n  ...\n]\n```\nCollect keys as strings. For removed/added sets, dedupe.\n\nFor codes: build map; for keys in frozen not in current → removed `codes:X`; in current not frozen → added; both → compare fields; changed.\n\ndraft_reason_mapping: keyed by draft.\n\nthresholds.tasks/fraction: key risk → compare op/value.\n\nordered: write_route_order, thresholds.priority → if JSON differs → changed with key `*`.\n\nNow checkFrozen: \n```js\nexport function checkFrozen(frozen, current) {\n  const diff = compareRegistry(frozen, current)\n  const errors = []\n  for (const d of diff.removed) errors.push({id:'freeze.removed', detail:d})\n  for (const d of diff.changed) errors.push({id:'freeze.changed', detail:d})\n  for (const d of diff.added) errors.push({id:'freeze.added', detail:d})\n  const ok = deepEqual(frozen, current)\n  if (!ok && errors.length===0 && !sameExceptVersion) errors.push({id:'freeze.version'...})\n```\nSpec: `freeze.version`：除 snapshot_version 外两者深相等，但版本号不同. So add version error only when that condition holds. If ok, no errors. If not ok and no other errors and version differs only → push version.\n\nLet me write: \n```js\nif (!ok) {\n  const a = {...frozen, snapshot_version: 0}... \n```\nSimpler