{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b6f3-1b9b-71cb-9a5e-52f58221bf74","session_id":"01a1b6ec-e364-796a-b544-183433e5f1dd","message":{"id":"01a1b6f31b9b71cb9a5e52f3e63f5578","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`.git/info/exclude` has `.collab/worktrees/` only. So `.collab/blueprint`, `.collab/tasks` are untracked and would appear in `git ls-files --others`. Unless they're tracked.\n\nLet me check the index by searching for a known path string in .git/index (binary but readable via strings). Use grep on binary? I can use `strings` equivalent. Let me check if `.collab/tasks/004/design.md` is in the index by searching bytes.\n\nActually simpler: `.git/worktrees/004/index` exists. Let me grep -c for \"tasks/004\" in it.\n\nNote: running `grep` on .git/index isn't a git command; acceptable.\n\nBut careful: If these are untracked, F-8 fails — then the reference implementation would fail too, unless check-write-scope ignores some paths... Design doesn't say ignore anything. Hmm.\n\nWait: maybe `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` from the worktree root only lists files under the worktree; `.collab/blueprint` is at root → listed. So F-8 would fail... unless the harness stages all files before review (state.json etc.), making them tracked. The orchestrator likely commits/stages task files. Indeed acceptance says \"必须先对新文件执行 git add <显式路径>\" for AC-073 — the orchestrator probably stages everything at the end, including .collab/tasks/004.\n\nBut F-8 with --base 93ebbb3: if `.collab/tasks/004/design.md` is tracked (added), it matches `.collab/tasks/004/**` ✓. What about `.collab/blueprint/*`? If tracked and unchanged since 93ebbb3, it won't appear in diff. Are they tracked? `.collab/blueprint` might be committed in the repo (the normal workflow: blueprint committed, worktrees ignored). That's plausible: the ignore for `.collab/worktrees/` implies the rest of `.collab` is tracked. \n\nAnd `.collab/tasks/001..003` likely tracked from earlier tasks. `.collab/tasks/004` is new → untracked now, but orchestrator will `git add` them (or they're expected to be untracked and matched by the extra scope).\n\nHmm, but if `.collab/tasks/004/*` remains untracked, F-8 still passes thanks to the extra scope. And `.collab/blueprint` tracked → not listed. So F-8 works. Good. Let me verify blueprint is tracked by grepping index. Actually let's not; the `--exclude-standard` will tell me when I run the check. But I can't run git...\n\nWait — my check-write-scope script itself runs `git` when invoked. Am I allowed? The prohibition is on me executing git commands. Running my script in a test would execute git. Hmm, H-3 runs tests which run git via spawn in temp dirs. That's part of the test suite, not me issuing git commands. But F-8 directly executes git through my script.\n\nI think the prohibition targets mutating git state / commits. To be safe, I'll avoid invoking git directly myself, but the deliverable's script must use git. When I self-test, I may run the check scripts — that indirectly runs git read-only commands (`diff --name-only`, `ls-files`). Is that \"执行 git 命令\"? Indirectly. Risky.\n\nHmm. I need to self-test to ensure acceptance passes. Read-only git invocations via my own script are arguably fine and necessary. But the instruction is explicit... The instruction likely exists to prevent dev from committing/manipulating. But \"执行 git 命令\" literally includes `git status`.\n\nCompromise: I'll implement and test using `--root` temp copies for golden checks (no git), and avoid invoking check-write-scope with --base. I can test registry-only mode (no git). For scope mode I can test with a temp git repo in os.tmpdir() created via `git init` — that's executing git.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider: the full test suite (H-3) will spawn git in temp dirs regardless — that's the acceptance's own requirement. The user forbids *me* from running git. I'll respect it literally: no direct `git` invocations from my shell. My scripts contain git calls; running them is a gray area, but I'll minimize: I'll verify logic by unit tests I write, and rely on acceptance.\n\nActually, I must at least verify F-7 (`node scripts/check-write-scope.mjs` with no args) — no git. And F-1/F-2/F-13/F-14/F-15/F-10/F-11/F-12 — no git. Only F-8/F-9/F-16/F-17 need git, plus spec-tracked tests. I'll skip direct verification of those but ensure code correctness.\n\nHmm, but spec-tracked tests will run in H-3 full test and use git. That's fine (not me).\n\nWait, but if files are untracked, test #36 fails and H-3 fails, making acceptance fail. The orchestrator is expected to git add. I'll note it in impl.md.\n\nOK. Now write the scripts.\n\ncheck-golden-frozen.mjs.\n\nFor hashing: \n```js\nfunction normalizedHash(buffer){\n  const text = buffer.toString('utf8').replace(/\\r\\n/g,'\\n');\n  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(text,'utf8').digest('hex');\n}\n```\n\nwalk dirs: recursively list files. Use fs.readdirSync withFileTypes.\n\nThen compare.\n\nManifest schema validation: load schemas/golden-manifest.schema.json; if load fails → skip? Must validate; if missing treat as manifest_invalid? I'll try; if schema missing, skip validation (temp copies include contracts dir fully so present).\n\nWrite manifest: {schema, files: sorted [{path, sha256}]} with 2-space indent + \\n.\n\nConsider --root and --write combination.\n\nNow check-write-scope needs manifest reading similarly.\n\nLet me write both plus gen/check doc, check-no-collab.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b6ece74172df9c584a52539772f3","_messageId":"01a1b6f31b9b71cb9a5e52f3e63f5578","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b6ece74172df9c584a52539772f3","codebuddy.ai/mode