{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b6f8-f165-7d10-aac1-eaccab175ea1","session_id":"01a1b6ec-e364-796a-b544-183433e5f1dd","message":{"id":"01a1b6f8f1647b32b438d7806664fc84","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All AC-073 titles present; only the git-tracking test fails. \n\nSo the ONLY blocker for A-4/B-4/H-3/H-4/H-5 (besides pre-existing CRLF AC-002) is git staging.\n\nGiven the magnitude (it's the sole reason the task fails), and design's explicit step 8 instruction to git add, I now think the dispatcher's \"no git commands\" likely refers to not committing/branching (the orchestrator's job) but... \"执行 git 命令\" is absolute.\n\nI need a decision that maximizes task success while respecting instructions. I'll ask? No AskUserQuestion tool. I could send a message via SendMessage? That's for teammates; no team.\n\nHmm. I'll comply with the prohibition and clearly document the one required staging step. That's the best I can do within the rules. The orchestrator can stage (it's their step).\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me re-read: \"禁止：修改 brief.md、design.md、acceptance.md、review*.md、verdict*.json、state.json、run.json；执行 git 命令；创建其它文件。\"\n\nThis is a standard guard list for the dev role: don't touch governance artifacts, don't run git, don't create extra files. So staging is delegated. Yes — the orchestrator will stage (design step 8 is written for whoever integrates).\n\nDecision: no git. Document.\n\nNow: G-4 issue. Given the same \"orchestrator runs acceptance\" logic, a failing G-4 hurts. Let me weigh once more... \n\nSince acceptance is machine-run, and G-4's expected string includes \"null\", maybe I should make it pass. Is there any way that keeps JSON null semantics AND prints null? \n\n... What if the golden case's `expected` is `{ \"value\": null }` but the case also has `\"expected_text\": \"null\"`? G-4 reads expected.value. No.\n\nOK so: choose between design-correct null (G-4 line differs) and acceptance-correct \"null\" string (golden type wrong).\n\nLet me