t exit non-zero.\n\nSo commands should be self-verifying: run the check, and if fine print \"AC-009 OK\" else exit 1.\n\nNote the runner treats each section heading as a check. Multiple sections per AC is allowed (\"Cover each AC you own with its own '## AC-xxx' section\" — at minimum one per AC). But I can have multiple sections all starting with the same AC id? Rule 1 says heading must start with AC id. Rule 4 says cover each AC with its own section. Probably simplest and safest: one section per AC — 4 sections: AC-009, AC-010, AC-011, AC-073. But then a huge command each. Single-line commands can be long though. Better: group related checks into one command per AC using a PowerShell one-liner with semicolons and `if` statements.\n\nHmm, but the acceptance.md is the reviewer's document describing all the assertions. If I collapse 52 checks into 4 giant one-liners, that's hard to read but complies.\n\nAlternative: multiple sections with same AC prefix, e.g. \"## AC-009 Test entry\", \"## AC-009 Registry equals snapshot\", ... That satisfies rule 1 (heading starts with AC id) and covers AC-009. Rule 4 says each AC gets its own section — multiple sections per AC still \"cover each AC\". This preserves granularity (52 checks), which is better for the review report.\n\nI think granular is better: keep all the original checks, but convert each into the required shape with heading `## AC-009 <title>` and a single-line `- 命令:` that self-verifies and prints \"AC-009 OK\".\n\nBut wait — a risk: if the runner does `Invoke-Expression` on the command and checks stdout contains \"AC-009 OK\", and exit 0. Multiple sections per AC, each printing \"AC-009 OK\" — that's fine; each check is judged independently.\n\nHmm, but \"期望\" lines — I should keep assertions there for human readability.\n\nHowever: 52 single-line commands, each wrapped in a single backtick pair. Backticks are also PowerShell escape char. If the command contains a backtick, it'd break the single backtick pair pa